<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="6-hydroxymethyl-dihydropterin pyrophosphate biosynthesis I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6147" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: DIHYDROPTERIN-CH2OH-PP| (6-hydroxymethyl-dihydropterin diphosphate) is the pterin precursor for the biosynthesis of several important cofactors, including |FRAME:THF|, |FRAME: CPD-10763| and |FRAME: CPD-10764|. The compound is always synthesized from GTP, but the enzymes involve may differ among organisms. This pathway, which is found in bacteria, fungi and plants, starts with |FRAME: FOLE-CPLX|, an enzyme that converts GTP into |FRAME: DIHYDRONEOPTERIN-P3|. The triphosphate motif is removed in a process that is still not completely understood, and the resulting |FRAME: DIHYDRO-NEO-PTERIN| is converted to |FRAME: AMINO-OH-HYDROXYMETHYL-DIHYDROPTERIDINE| by |FRAME: CPLX0-3936| (FolB). In plants the pterin moiety is formed from GTP in the cytosol.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
  </Authors>
